  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting your log home's interior ready for staining is crucial for a beautiful, long-lasting finish. One of the biggest factors is making sure the wood is dry. If you try to stain logs that still have a high moisture content, the stain won't penetrate properly and you'll end up with an uneven look or even adhesion issues down the line. It's not just about the logs feeling dry to the touch; professional moisture meters can give you a more accurate reading. Beyond moisture, you need to ensure the surface is clean. Any mold or mildew needs to be addressed and thoroughly cleaned before you even think about applying stain. Don't skip this step! Also, think about any previous coatings. If there's an old stain or sealer, you'll likely need to remove it or at least ensure it's compatible with your new product. Applying new stain over an incompatible old coating can lead to peeling or a splotchy appearance. For large projects or if you're unsure about the moisture content or proper prep, it's always a good idea to chat with an expert like HH Remodeling LLC. We can help you assess the condition of your logs and get them ready for a perfect stain."
    }
  ],
